      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C06"],
      "claim": "D-histidine also partitions into rat brain with continuously increasing brain/plasma ratios, showing that even non-canonical stereoisomers of azole amino acids achieve CNS exposure after systemic administration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C07"],
      "claim": "FDOPA, a small aromatic amino acid analogue, enters the brain via carrier-mediated transport at the blood-brain barrier, reinforcing that transporter-mediated uptake can provide meaningful CNS access for small polar acid-containing molecules.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a small azole carboxylic acid closely related to imidazole-4-acetic acid, which has demonstrated in vivo BBB penetration and CNS effects. Multiple high-transfer analogues, including histidine and fluorinated amino acid tracers, confirm that small azole/amino-acid-like molecules achieve meaningful CNS access via passive and transporter-mediated routes. No evidence contradicts CNS access, so the prediction is pass.",
